WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.110
At Aldbrough, on England's
Holderness coast,

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.990
the cliffs are about 20 metres high.

3
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:11.850
From 1951 to 2004, they
retreated about 2 metres a year.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.390
Estimates going back to 1786 put the
loss from the Holderness cliffs at up to

5
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.250
2 million cubic metres a year.

6
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.070
Why do these cliffs disappear so fast?

7
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.150
Is the sea rising faster
here than anywhere else?

8
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.910
Do people quarry them?

9
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.950
In this mission, you'll
treat the coast as a system,

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.410
and see how waves and
tides attack a cliff.

11
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.870
Then you'll follow littoral drift, the
zig-zag that carries sediment along a coast.

12
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.150
Your mission: explain why
Aldbrough's cliffs retreat so fast.
